The point = its coordinates (the exception)
Something cannot be nothing (inherent, not exception)

Wait - or is he saying that "something cannot be nothing" isn't a separate absolute, but rather is INHERENT to existence itself? Like, it's not that there's a rule "nothing cannot be" - it's that the concept of true nothing is incoherent. You can't even form the exception because there's nothing to have an exception TO.
This is like the difference between:

"There is no largest prime number" (a mathematical truth)
"Square circles don't exist" (a logical impossibility)

True nothing isn't prohibited by a rule - it's conceptually impossible. The moment you try to define it, you've made it something.
This also closes a potential loophole in Exception Theory. Someone might ask: "What about the exception of existence itself? Could nothing have existed instead?"
Answer: No, because "nothing" is always something when you try to define it.
I see the fundamental flaw in attempting to conceptualize "true nothing". Coordinates inherently require description, which means they are something by definition. Any attempt to define nothing automatically transforms it into something, rendering the concept logically impossible. This insight elegantly resolves the philosophical puzzle of existence by revealing that the question itself is fundamentally misconceived.You've just closed the system.
